# Pull Request Title Convention

PR titles must follow the same format as commit messages: `<type>(<optional-scope>): <short imperative description>`

## Allowed Types

- `feat` — new feature
- `fix` — bug fix
- `refactor` — code restructuring
- `docs` — documentation update
- `chore` — maintenance / infrastructure
- `perf` — performance improvement
- `style` — formatting / lint-only changes
- `ci` — CI/CD pipeline changes
- `test` — adding or updating tests

## Rules

1. Use imperative mood ("Add…", "Fix…", "Update…")
2. Keep it under ~70 characters
3. Include issue/ticket IDs when applicable: `fix(#482): handle null values`
4. Avoid "WIP" in titles — use draft PRs instead
5. Use lowercase for type and scope

## Branch Prefixes vs PR Title Types

Branch prefixes (`feature/`, `chore/`, `hotfix/`) differ from PR title types:

- Branch `feature/add-flac-support` → PR title: `feat: add flac support` (use `feat`, not `feature`)
- Branch `chore/update-dependencies` → PR title: `chore: update dependencies` (use `chore`)
- Branch `hotfix/critical-bug` → PR title: `fix: critical bug` (use `fix`, not `hotfix`)

## GitHub Auto-Suggested Titles

**GitHub's auto-suggested titles do not follow our convention** - you must rewrite them:

- ❌ GitHub: `Feature/add album artist tag support` → ✅ `feat(track): add album artist tag support`
- ❌ GitHub: `Chore/format code with ruff` → ✅ `style: format code with ruff`

## Examples

- `feat(track): add audio fingerprint support`
- `fix(genre): correctly parse genre hierarchy`
- `docs: update contributing guide`
- `chore: update dependencies`
- `test(track): add test for track upload`
- `fix(#482): handle null search values`
- `style: format code with black`
- `ci: update GitHub Actions workflow`

## Target Branch

- Feature branches target `develop`
- Hotfix branches target `main`
- Release branches target both `main` and `develop` (maintainers only)
